A||||16||FNXRISING||Torque converter relay... OK to drive w/o?||||1||
Z||000000||FNXRISING||01-05-2001||11:50 AM||||I have also been having my 88 GT stalling, usually right after getting off of the freeway.  If I put it in neutral, it is OK.  But upon putting it in drive, it dies immediately.  Several on the forum suggested the TCC relay might be the problem in a related post.  Question:  is it OK to drive around with the relay unplugged without being detrimental?  If so, what is the actual function of it?<P>TIA!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dj_yellow88-catiny_3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||afRaceR||01-05-2001||12:35 PM||||If your driving just around town with very little highway speeds then you might be ok. Otherwise you need to replace the TCC solenoid as quickly as possible.<BR>If you don't, you will be buying a whole new transmission because your's will burn up. At higher speeds, without the lockup engaged, the oil gets to hot and will eventually burn the oil up.<BR>Its hard to say how long you can drive it like this. Some people can make it a week some a couple of months.||||reg||1
Z||000002||KRMFiero||01-05-2001||03:35 PM||||If you disconnect it you weill get bad gas milage   (Well My friends 2.8 Oldsmobiel does when he took his off)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||01-05-2001||03:53 PM||||Better spring for the cash and get it fised ASAP! Tranny will burn up very fast!||||reg||1
Z||000004||dman||01-05-2001||04:38 PM||||Hello everyone. FNXRISING, if you are mechanically inclined you can perform the fix yourself. Here are couple of links for you in case you don't already know the procedure:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.kichline.com/chuck/fiero/TCC.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.kichline.com/chuck/fiero/TCC.htm</A>   <A HREF="http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/tcc.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/tcc.html</A>  <P>Don't forget to check out theogre's cave for a good article on the TH125C transmission.<P>Last time I changed mine I took the time to flush the ATF fluid and change the filter. I used the technique that theogre describes in his cave article. Worked great. This way I didn't have to worry about ATF dripping everywhere when I took off the side cover.<P>The FieroStore now stocks the TCC solenoid; I buy mine from a local trannie shop, from there the cost is like 12 bucks and another buck or two for the gasket.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Raydar||01-07-2001||10:12 PM||||I disagree that having it disconnected will harm the trans. Mine has been disconnected for over a year with no ill effects, other than crappy gas mileage. (I do plan to replace the solenoid. It's just not a priority.) <BR>Have an ex-g/f with the same tranny (125C) in a 3.1 Lumina. She's probably put 100,000 miles on hers with the TCC solenoid disconnected, with no problems. <BR>If it was a 4-speed auto, I would think it would be a different story. In 4th gear (O.D.), you might be driving below the stall speed of the converter, causing much more slippage and heat. <BR>Just an opinion. <p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 01-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||01-08-2001||12:07 AM||||Anything that wastes gas is bad.<P>How much if any damage you'll do depends on a few things.<P>I wouldn't run it like that any more than I had to. Just for the fuel economy issue if nothing else.<P>There's recent updates to the notes in my cave about this. <A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Fiero Cave</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Hank is Here||01-08-2001||10:56 AM||||This is not in the Fiero application but rather similar.  My winter driver is a 88 Chevy Celebrity with 2.8 and a TH125C. Only real difference is that this car has DIS ignition.<P>About three years ago the tcc started going, so I had it disconnected.  I have driven it about 25,000 miles with it disconnected.  No ill effects!!  The car now gets about 25 mpg highway and 23 around town.  It was getting 27 before the soleniod was disconnected.  The darn thing still kills my Fiero on fuel milage with it being heavier and me driving it harder.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||ChadMan||01-08-2001||11:36 AM||||Why would driving around with the TCC disconnected burn up the transmission?  Prior to the early 80's, automatic transmissions didn't have lockup torque convertors, and they didn't burn up.<P>(This is an honest question from a manual-tranny guy, not a sarcastic question from a smartass.)||||reg||5
Z||000009||StuGood||01-08-2001||12:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Hank is Here:<BR><B>...have driven it about 25,000 miles with it disconnected.  No ill effects!!  The car now gets about 25 mpg highway and 23 around town.  It was getting 27 before...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>2 mpg is pretty significant - over three years of driving, it adds up.  How much $ and downtime are we talking about, to fix the TCC?  Probably you'll find it's worth your time to fix it.  Disconnecting the solenoid is just a temporary fix.  Why waste fuel and possibly shorten your trans. life, if it's easy to fix? <P>As far as why a trans. would burn up w/o the TCC, any slippage in the torque converter generates heat.  If you have slippage all the time, you have higher temperatures in the trans. and shorter trans. life,  all else being equal.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Carrolles||01-08-2001||01:26 PM||||Hank,<P>I had an 86 Pontiac 6000 with the 2.8l and the th125 trans.  It also did pretty good on mileage.  I think the final drive gear ratio is a bit taller on the Celebrity/6000 than the Fiero.  My 85SE v-6 4spd has a final drive of around 3.63:1.  The other cars probably have around 3.30:1 or which could explain the mileage.<P>Take care,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000011||Boxdin||01-08-2001||06:40 PM||||I agree with ChadMan. My family had two of the 1980 Olds Omega (first front drive) with v6 and th125 autos. These cars were driven lots of miles over many years and never had a trans failure. The lockup came years later and is useful, but not mandatory. <P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||01-08-2001||08:04 PM||||The old non locking TC's are built to diferant internal clearances than the lockers are. Locking TC's are built to get higher torque multiply when unlocked.<P>You guys run them however you want. It might not break but you will pay for it. Losing 2-3mpg on a 10 gallon tank blows 20-30 miles off the range of a tank.... That is at least a gallon of gas wasted in each fillup. Do the math. The TC solinoid gets really cheap fast when gas is running $1.50 or more a gallon. Pays back even faster in Canada or Europe.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Tweek||01-09-2001||12:07 AM||||Just a note here - Mine ('84) has been disconnected for about the past months with no problems yet. But that is all city driving. I'm driving down to CT tomorrow (a couple of hundred miles) and back on Thursday. I'll report on any problems and the ave gas milage....<P>-Ted||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||01-09-2001||07:55 PM||||To use hank as an example.<P>25,000 miles @ 27MPG uses 926 gallons (Base w/ TC lock)<BR>25,000 miles @ 25MPG uses 1000 gallons (2 MPG lost) 74 gallons wasted<BR>25,000 miles @ 24MPG uses 1042 gallons (3 MPG lost) 116 gallons wasted<P>74*$1.50 per gallon = $111.00 lost<BR>116*$1.50 per gallon = $174.00 lost<P>If you do that at $2.00 per gallon like some people paid last summer and are still close to...<P>74*$2.00 per gallon = $148.00 lost<BR>116*$2.00 per gallon = $232.00 lost<P>I can think of other things I'd rather have that money for.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Hank is Here||01-09-2001||08:05 PM||||Just to state why I haven't fixed my tcc.  The car it is in is a 88' Chevy Celebrity, not exactly a collectors car.  At the time it my tcc died I had to park the car I was so poor, and also because I did not know it was the tcc.  I went from about 27 to the gallon in that to my 66 jeep which at the time got about 7 mpg out of a 4 banger for a few months till I could get it fixed.  Also when this happened I was buying gas for about $.79  or so, so 2 mpg did not make a difference. At least now I get over 20 mpg out of the same jeep    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR> <P>[This message has been edited by Hank is Here (edited 01-09-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Hank is Here (edited 01-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||theogre||01-10-2001||09:36 AM||||I wasn't picking on you. Your numbers above just happened to be convenient to run the numbers on. I'm just trying to give everyone the real cost of running w/o the TC locking for long periods in the current economy.<P>If you figure most people drive 10000-15000 miles a year it's still a good chunk of money. The solinoid pays back pretty quick if you do it yourself. (It's time consuming more than dificult.) It will take longer to pay back if you pay to have it done. Lets face it... gas prices aren't likely to drop much anytime soon.||||reg||1
